{
  "id": "Compound::DB00762 - Disease::DOID:10534",
  "verbalization": "Irinotecan, an antineoplastic agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of stomach cancer through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Irinotecan may contribute to the development of stomach cancer via the induction of toxic effects in the gastrointestinal tract, as indicated by its association with Megacolon Toxic and Neutropenic Colitis. These conditions, in turn, are linked to other agents such as Methotrexate and Docetaxel, which are also used in the treatment of stomach cancer. The shared antineoplastic properties of Irinotecan and these agents, along with their common ancestry in the class of polyatomic ions and heteroatomic molecular entities, support a potential role in the pathophysiology of stomach cancer. The integration of these pathways highlights the complex interplay between chemotherapy-induced toxicity and cancer progression, emphasizing the importance of understanding the molecular mechanisms underlying drug-induced adverse effects.",
